==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|mēu|mēw|mēau|mēaw}}, {{alter|ang|mǣƿ}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*maiwiz}}, a byform of {{der|ang|gem-pro|*maiwaz}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|meau}}, {{m|fy|miuw}}, {{cog|osx|mēu}} (Middle Low German {{m|gml|meve}}, from which German {{m|de|Möwe}}), Dutch {{m|nl|meeuw}}, Old Norse {{m|non|már}}, {{m|non|mór}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|ang|/mæːw/}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|head=mǣw|m}}

# [[gull]], [[seagull]]
#: {{ux|ang|On gōdum dæġe ic ġefō crabban and telle '''mǣwas'''.|On a good day, I catch crabs and count '''seagulls'''.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Mīn ealdfæder samnode '''mǣwa''' ǣġru oþ þæt ǣne dæġe sume mǣwas his ēagan ūt āhaccodon.|My grandfather used to collect '''seagull''' eggs until one day some '''seagulls''' pecked out his eyes.}}

